+# On-call runbook
+
+For whoever holds the pager for tally. Where a decision changed, this page follows the latest one.
+
+## Before your first shift
+
+- The pager app is **Beacon** (blue owl icon). Install it and log in with SSO before your first shift.
+- The command line tool is `tallyctl`. Its old name, `tally-cli`, still works as an alias for one more release and then
+  goes away, so use `tallyctl`, including in scripts.
+- Since the 2026-09 release the service listens on port **8731**. Port 8080 is closed.
+- While you handle an incident, write down each command as you run it. This rule is in the on-call handover notes.
+
+## Escalation path
+
+| Step | Who gets paged | When |
+|------|----------------|------|
+| 1 | Primary on-call | Immediately |
+| 2 | Secondary on-call | After 15 minutes without acknowledgement from the primary |
+| 3 | Engineering manager (currently Mara) | After 15 minutes without acknowledgement from the secondary |
+
+The wait is 15 minutes, not 30. The 30-minute rule from 2 September was dropped on 16 September, and the pager
+configuration already uses 15. The manager step is a real step, not a courtesy. Acknowledging is a tap on the phone,
+so do it right away, before you reach a laptop.
+
+**Security incidents** don't go through the secondary. Page the security lead directly.
+
+## When to page at night
+
+Between **22:00 and 07:00** the pager is for **severity 1** only:
+
+- **Severity 1:** customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Page, at any hour.
+- **Severity 2:** for example a slow dashboard or a delayed batch. No page at night. It waits for business hours and
+  the primary picks it up in the morning.
+
+This rule covers the pager only. It is not permission to ignore the dashboards.
+
+## Restarting a service
+
+Check the state of every service first with `tallyctl status` (add `--watch` to refresh every two seconds). Then
+restart one service at a time:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart api
+tallyctl restart ingest
+tallyctl restart worker
+tallyctl restart scheduler
+```
+
+- If the ingest queue is stuck, use `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` instead of the plain restart (see below).
+  `--drain` works for ingest only.
+- `--timeout SECONDS` makes a restart give up after that many seconds. The default is 120.
+- To follow the logs of one service, run `tallyctl logs tail --follow --service NAME`.
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stuck
+
+**Signs:** the queue depth alert fires. The ingest workers are running and look healthy, but the queue doesn't
+drain, and the oldest message has been sitting there for hours.
+
+**Cause:** a malformed ("poisoned") batch has wedged the consumer, which keeps retrying it. It is not the network,
+which cost half an hour of checking in August.
+
+**Fix:** `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` first, and look for the cause afterwards. A plain restart doesn't help,
+because the consumer picks up the same batch again. The drain finishes in-flight work, parks the stuck batch in the
+dead-letter table and starts the consumer clean. Inspect the parked batch
+the next day. A permanent fix for the poisoned batch was still in review on 30 September.
+
+### Database host disk full
+
+**Signs:** the disk alert fires at 80 percent. At 97 percent the database starts refusing writes. Retry logs and
+table bloat after a backlog are typical causes.
+
+**Fix, in this order:**
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+The vacuum needs a little scratch space and the rotation doesn't, so vacuum first. `--online` doesn't block writers
+and is the one to use in production. A plain vacuum locks the tables and made things worse in August. `--full`
+needs the service stopped. `logs rotate` compresses old log files and removes those past retention. It keeps the
+last 14 by default (`--keep N`). In August this took the disk from 97 to 61 percent, and in September from 85 to 55
+percent.
+
+### API certificate expired
+
+**Signs:** the public API answers with certificate errors, and the external synthetic check fails. In September the
+load balancer, DNS and the latest deployment were checked first, and none was at fault.
+
+**Fix:**
+
+```
+tallyctl certs list
+tallyctl certs renew api
+tallyctl restart api
+```
+
+Read the expiry date in the listing. Renewing takes two steps: the service keeps serving the old certificate until
+you restart it, so the restart is required. The same goes for the ingest certificate (`tallyctl certs renew ingest`,
+then `tallyctl restart ingest`). Use `--dry-run` on the renewal to see what it would do. The renewal job now raises
+an alert when it fails. The alert on days left before expiry isn't built yet, so check `tallyctl certs list` yourself.
+
+### Connection refused on port 8080
+
+Anything still talking to 8080, such as health checks in old scripts or bookmarks, fails with a refused connection.
+Point it at 8731. Every `tallyctl` command uses 8731 unless `--host` says otherwise.
+
+### Cache needs clearing / unknown subcommand
+
+The old purge command was removed in the 2026-09 release and now fails with an unknown-subcommand error. Use:
+
+```
+tallyctl cache clear
+tallyctl cache clear --region NAME
+```
+
+The first clears all regions, the second only one. Clearing the cache doesn't free memory and isn't part of the fix
+for a stuck queue or a full disk. Someone tried that in August, and it did nothing useful.
